# ======================= 2.3 NEAREST NEIGHBOUR =======================
A['2.3']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.29–30 (4.6 Quantum algorithms for nearest-neighbour search: Grover-based steps, amplitude-estimation steps), p.26–27 (quantum distance measurement in quantum k-means), p.23 (types of QML), p.37 (question bank item 5)",blocks=[
("def","**Nearest Neighbour Search (NNS)** finds the data point in a dataset that is **closest to a query point** under a distance measure (Euclidean, Hamming, cosine). It is the core of **k-NN classification**, recommendation and clustering. **Quantum NNS** uses **superposition** to evaluate distances to all points at once and **Grover-type amplitude amplification / amplitude estimation** to extract the nearest one (QAI-ii.pdf p.29)."),
("h","1. Classical vs quantum"),
("t",["Aspect","Classical NNS","Quantum NNS"],[["Distance evaluations","One point at a time: O(N·d)","All N points in superposition"],["Finding the minimum","Linear scan O(N)","Grover / minimum finding: O(√N)"],["Speed-up","–","**Quadratic** (book p.29)"],["Requirement","RAM","Quantum state preparation (QRAM)"]],[1.7,2.4,2.7]),
("h","2. Grover-based quantum NNS – steps (book p.29)"),
("p","**Input:** dataset of N points {x₁ … x_N} and a query point q.  **Output:** the data point closest to q."),
("n",["**Initialise:** prepare an index register in uniform superposition (1/√N) Σᵢ |i⟩ using Hadamards, and load the points: (1/√N) Σᵢ |i⟩|xᵢ⟩ (amplitude / basis encoding).",
"**Distance computation:** apply a quantum distance circuit (e.g. the **swap test**, which estimates the overlap |⟨xᵢ|q⟩|², or an arithmetic circuit) so that the distance dᵢ = D(xᵢ, q) is stored: (1/√N) Σᵢ |i⟩|dᵢ⟩ – all N distances computed in one pass.",
"**Oracle:** define an oracle that marks (phase −1) the index whose distance is below the current best threshold – i.e. the data point closest to the query.",
"**Grover iterations:** perform ≈ (π/4)√N Grover iterations to amplify the amplitude of the marked point.",
"**Measure** the index register → obtain the marked (nearest) point; update the threshold and repeat a few times (**Dürr–Høyer minimum finding**, O(√N) overall); verify classically."]),
("d",dg('a[label="Dataset x₁…x_N\\n+ query q"]; b[label="Encode\\n(1/√N) Σ|i⟩|xᵢ⟩"]; c[label="Distance circuit\\n(swap test) Σ|i⟩|dᵢ⟩"]; d[label="Oracle: mark\\ndᵢ < threshold",fillcolor="#FFF2CC"]; e[label="Grover\\namplification ≈ √N",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; f[label="Measure index\\n→ nearest point",shape=oval]; a->b->c->d->e->f; f->d[label="lower threshold, repeat",constraint=false];',"LR"),"Quantum nearest-neighbour search using Grover amplification",6.4),
("p","**Blocks:** *Encode* loads all points at once; *distance circuit* evaluates every distance in parallel; *oracle* only marks the candidate; *Grover* raises its probability; *measurement* returns it; the loop lowers the threshold until the minimum is found."),
("h","3. Amplitude-estimation method (book p.29–30)"),
("n",["Prepare a quantum state representing all data points and the query.","Apply amplitude amplification to boost the nearest-neighbour state.","Use **amplitude estimation** to estimate that amplitude (it encodes closeness).","Measure → the data point with the highest amplitude = nearest neighbour."]),
("h","4. Worked example"),
("t",["Point","Coordinates","Distance to q = (1, 1)"],[["A","(0, 0)","√2 = 1.41"],["B","(1, 2)","**1.00 (nearest)**"],["C","(4, 1)","3.00"],["D","(5, 5)","√32 = 5.66"]],[1.0,1.5,2.6]),
("n",["N = 4 points → 2 index qubits: (|00⟩ + |01⟩ + |10⟩ + |11⟩)/2 for A, B, C, D.","Distances computed in superposition; oracle marks |01⟩ (B, distance 1.00 is the smallest).","For N = 4 and one marked item, **one** Grover iteration rotates the state exactly onto |01⟩ → P(B) = 1.","Measurement gives 01 → **B is the nearest neighbour**. Classically, all 4 distances must be compared."]),
("h","5. Applications and limitations"),
("b",["**Applications:** k-NN classification (label of nearest neighbours), recommendation systems, image and pattern recognition, document retrieval, quantum k-means (same distance routine, p.26–27).","**Limitations:** needs efficient **data loading (QRAM)** – otherwise loading costs O(N) and cancels the gain; only quadratic speed-up; probabilistic output; noisy NISQ hardware."]),
("concl","Quantum NNS computes all distances in superposition and uses Grover amplification (or amplitude estimation) to pick the closest point in O(√N) steps – a quadratic speed-up over a classical scan."),
("tip","**“Encode – Distance – Mark – Amplify – Measure.”** Say *quadratic* speed-up and mention the swap test for distance."),
])
# ======================= 2.4 BOOSTING =======================
A['2.4']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.30–31 (quantum boosting: idea of combining weak learners, fundamental steps 1–6), p.23 (quantum boosting in types of QML)",blocks=[
("def","**Boosting** is an ensemble technique that combines many **weak learners** (models only slightly better than random guessing) into one **strong learner** by training them one after another and focusing each new learner on the examples the previous ones got wrong. **Quantum boosting** brings quantum computing into this process – encoding data in quantum states and using quantum parallelism to evaluate and improve weak learners faster (QAI-ii.pdf p.30)."),
("h","1. Idea"),
("b",["“Boosting is like combining the strengths of many weak learners to create a powerful team” (book p.30).","Quantum computers can **work on many possibilities at once**, potentially making boosting more efficient and accurate for certain problems."]),
("h","2. Fundamental steps of quantum boosting (book p.31)"),
("n",["**Weak learner selection:** choose a set of weak learners (decision stumps/trees, small neural networks) to form the ensemble.","**Quantum data representation:** encode the training data into quantum states, using superposition for parallel processing.","**Quantum operations:** apply quantum gates and circuits to evaluate/improve the weak learners (e.g. compute weighted errors of many hypotheses in superposition).","**Measurement and weighting:** measure to obtain classical results; assign each weak learner a weight according to its accuracy.","**Weighted combination:** combine the weak learners' predictions in a weighted manner to form the strong classifier.","**Iterations:** repeat, adjusting the emphasis on examples according to past performance (mis-classified examples get more weight)."]),
("d",dg('a[label="Training data\\n(equal weights wᵢ)"]; b[label="Encode in\\nquantum states"]; c[label="Quantum evaluation /\\nsearch of weak learners",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; d[label="Measure → best hₜ\\nweight αₜ"]; e[label="Update example\\nweights wᵢ"]; f[label="Strong learner\\nH(x) = sign(Σ αₜ hₜ(x))",shape=oval]; a->b->c->d->e; e->c[label="next round",constraint=false]; d->f[style=dashed,label="after T rounds"];',"LR"),"Quantum boosting loop",6.4),
("p","**Blocks:** data weights start equal; the quantum block evaluates many candidate weak learners at once (e.g. Grover/amplitude estimation picks the one with lowest weighted error); measurement gives the chosen learner and its weight; example weights are updated; after T rounds the weighted vote is the strong classifier."),
("h","3. Formulas (AdaBoost weighting used in the combination)"),
("f",["Weighted error:  εₜ = Σᵢ wᵢ · [hₜ(xᵢ) ≠ yᵢ]","Learner weight:  αₜ = ½ ln((1 − εₜ)/εₜ)","Example update:  wᵢ ← wᵢ · e^(−αₜ yᵢ hₜ(xᵢ)), then normalise so Σ wᵢ = 1","Strong classifier:  H(x) = sign( Σₜ αₜ hₜ(x) )"]),
("p","Symbols: xᵢ = training example, yᵢ ∈ {−1, +1} = true label, wᵢ = example weight, hₜ = weak learner of round t, αₜ = its vote strength, T = number of rounds."),
("h","4. Worked example – spam filter"),
("t",["Round","Weak learner hₜ","Weighted error εₜ","Weight αₜ"],[["1","“contains the word FREE”","0.30","½ ln(0.70/0.30) = **0.42**"],["2","“more than 5 links” (focus on mistakes of round 1)","0.25","½ ln(0.75/0.25) = **0.55**"],["3","“sender not in contacts”","0.20","½ ln(0.80/0.20) = **0.69**"]],[0.7,2.9,1.3,1.9]),
("p","Final rule: H(x) = sign(0.42 h₁ + 0.55 h₂ + 0.69 h₃). An e-mail flagged by h₂ and h₃ but not h₁ gets score −0.42 + 0.55 + 0.69 = 0.82 > 0 → **spam**. The quantum part speeds up step ‘find the stump with the lowest weighted error among thousands of candidate stumps’."),
("h","5. Advantages and limitations"),
("b",["**Advantages:** faster search over large hypothesis spaces (quadratic via amplitude amplification); handles high-dimensional data in superposition; robust ensemble accuracy; hybrid (fits NISQ era).","**Limitations:** data loading cost; noise in quantum evaluation; still largely theoretical; sensitive to noisy labels like classical boosting."]),
("concl","Quantum boosting keeps the boosting recipe – weak learners, weights, weighted vote – and uses quantum data representation and parallel evaluation to select and weight learners faster."),
("tip","**“Select – Encode – Evaluate (quantum) – Weigh – Combine – Repeat.”** Write α = ½ ln((1 − ε)/ε)."),
])
# ======================= 2.8 LINEAR ALGEBRA =======================
A['2.8']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.23–24 (4.3 Quantum algorithms for linear algebra: HHL problem, conditions, steps 1–6, complexity; quantum matrix multiplication, eigenvalue estimation, SVD, polynomial solvers, PCA, QSVT), p.21 (QPE, QSVE)",blocks=[
("def","**Linear algebra** deals with matrices and vectors (solving Ax = b, multiplying matrices, eigenvalues). **Quantum algorithms for linear algebra** use superposition, phase estimation and amplitude encoding to perform such operations **exponentially faster in some cases** – they are the engine of many quantum machine-learning methods (QAI-ii.pdf p.23)."),
("h","1. HHL algorithm (Harrow, Hassidim, Lloyd – 2009)"),
("b",["**Problem:** solve A x = b where A is an N×N **Hermitian** matrix (book p.23).","**Conditions for exponential speed-up:** A is **sparse** and **well-conditioned** (small condition number κ), and we need only a **measurement/expectation value** on x, not every entry of x."]),
("h","2. Steps of HHL (book p.23–24)"),
("n",["**Amplitude encoding:** prepare |b⟩ = Σ bᵢ|i⟩/‖b‖ (log₂N qubits hold N numbers).","**Hamiltonian simulation:** build the unitary e^(iAt) from A.","**Quantum Phase Estimation (QPE):** writing |b⟩ = Σⱼ βⱼ|uⱼ⟩ in A's eigenbasis, QPE stores eigenvalues: Σⱼ βⱼ |uⱼ⟩|λⱼ⟩.","**Ancilla rotation:** rotate an ancilla qubit by an angle depending on 1/λⱼ: Σⱼ βⱼ|uⱼ⟩|λⱼ⟩(√(1 − C²/λⱼ²)|0⟩ + (C/λⱼ)|1⟩).","**Inverse QPE:** uncompute the eigenvalue register.","**Measure** the ancilla; when it is 1, the system is in |x⟩ ∝ Σⱼ (βⱼ/λⱼ)|uⱼ⟩ = A⁻¹|b⟩; then measure the needed property of x."]),
("d",dg('a[label="|b⟩\\namplitude encoding"]; b[label="e^(iAt)\\nHamiltonian simulation"]; c[label="QPE\\n→ eigenvalues λⱼ",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; d[label="Ancilla rotation\\n∝ 1/λⱼ",fillcolor="#FFF2CC"]; e[label="Inverse QPE\\n(uncompute)"]; f[label="Measure ancilla = 1\\n|x⟩ = A⁻¹|b⟩",shape=oval]; a->b->c->d->e->f;',"LR"),"Block diagram of the HHL algorithm",6.4),
("p","**Blocks:** encoding loads b; Hamiltonian simulation turns A into a unitary; QPE extracts eigenvalues; the ancilla rotation performs the ‘division by λ’ (matrix inversion); inverse QPE cleans up; post-selected measurement leaves the solution state."),
("h","3. Complexity (book p.24)"),
("t",["Method","Time complexity"],[["Classical (conjugate gradient, sparse A)","O(N · κ)"],["Quantum HHL","O(log N · κ²)  – polylogarithmic in N"]],[3.4,3.0]),
("p","Symbols: N = size of the system, κ = condition number (largest/smallest eigenvalue ratio of A)."),
("h","4. Other quantum linear-algebra algorithms (book p.24)"),
("t",["Algorithm","Purpose"],[["Quantum matrix multiplication","Faster products using parallelism in special cases"],["Quantum eigenvalue / eigenvector estimation (QPE)","Eigen-decomposition; quantum chemistry"],["Quantum Singular Value Decomposition / QSVE","Low-rank approximation, recommendation systems"],["Quantum Principal Component Analysis (QPCA)","Dimensionality reduction"],["Quantum polynomial-system solvers","Systems of polynomial equations"],["Quantum Singular Value Transformation (QSVT)","Unifying framework applying functions to singular values"]],[3.0,3.8]),
("h","5. Applications and limitations"),
("b",["**Applications:** least-squares regression, SVM training, PCA, recommendation systems, solving differential equations, finance and simulation.","**Limitations:** state preparation (QRAM) and read-out costs; output is a quantum state, not a full vector; depends on sparsity and κ; deep circuits need fault tolerance; some speed-ups ‘dequantized’ by quantum-inspired classical algorithms."]),
("concl","Quantum linear algebra – led by HHL with QPE and amplitude encoding – can solve linear systems and related problems in time polylogarithmic in matrix size under suitable conditions, powering quantum machine learning."),
("tip","**HHL = “Encode – Simulate – QPE – Rotate (1/λ) – un-QPE – Measure”**; complexity O(log N κ²) vs O(N κ)."),
])
# ======================= 2.2 SVM vs QSVM =======================
A['2.2']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.36 (Case study: performance comparison of classical SVM and QSVM – effectiveness, speed, resource requirements, summary), p.31–35 (QSVM working and Qiskit example), p.22 (QSVM overview)",blocks=[
("def","A **classical Support Vector Machine (SVM)** finds the **maximum-margin hyperplane** separating classes, using the **kernel trick** for non-linear data. A **Quantum SVM (QSVM)** maps data into quantum states with a **quantum feature map** (an exponentially large Hilbert space) and uses quantum circuits to compute kernels/find the separating hyperplane (QAI-ii.pdf p.31, 36)."),
("h","1. Key differences (book case study p.36 + standard points)"),
("t",["Basis","Classical SVM","Quantum SVM (QSVM)"],[
["Data representation","Real feature vectors in Rᵈ","Quantum states |φ(x)⟩ of qubits"],
["Feature space","Kernel-induced; explicit features grow with degree","Hilbert space of dimension 2ⁿ for n qubits (quantum feature map, e.g. ZZ map)"],
["Kernel computation","Classical function K(x, y) (RBF, polynomial)","Quantum kernel K(x, y) = |⟨φ(x)|φ(y)⟩|² estimated on a circuit"],
["Effectiveness","Powerful for linearly separable / moderate data; may overfit or struggle with very high-dimensional complex patterns","Can handle high-dimensional data efficiently; may separate patterns hard for classical kernels"],
["Speed","Training/prediction time grows with dataset size and kernel complexity (≈ O(N²)–O(N³))","Potential speed-up via quantum parallelism (claimed polylog for least-squares QSVM); NISQ noise may cancel it"],
["Resource requirements","Large memory/compute for big datasets; quadratic optimisation","Qubits and gates for encoding; error correction for large problems"],
["Optimisation","Convex QP → global optimum","Hybrid: quantum kernel + classical optimiser (or variational U(θ))"],
["Output","Deterministic","Probabilistic (repeated shots)"],
["Hardware & maturity","Mature libraries (scikit-learn), CPUs/GPUs","Early stage; simulators and small noisy devices"],
["Interpretability","Support vectors, weights well understood","Harder to interpret quantum feature space"],
["Best suited for","Small/medium datasets today","High-dimensional or quantum-advantage problems in future"]],[1.5,2.6,2.7]),
("h","2. Advantages of each"),
("t",["Classical SVM – advantages","QSVM – advantages"],[["Well-established, reliable and accurate today","Exponentially large feature space from few qubits"],["Convex optimisation → unique global solution","Kernels that are hard to compute classically"],["Cheap hardware, mature tools","Possible quantum speed-up in training and kernel evaluation"],["Easy to tune and interpret","Natural for quantum data (e.g. physics, chemistry)"]],[3.4,3.4]),
("h","3. Pipeline comparison"),
("d",dg('subgraph cluster_c {label="Classical SVM"; style=rounded; c1[label="Data x"]; c2[label="Kernel K(x,y)\\n(CPU)"]; c3[label="QP solver\\n→ hyperplane"]; c1->c2->c3;} subgraph cluster_q {label="Quantum SVM"; style=rounded; q1[label="Data x"]; q2[label="Quantum feature\\nmap |φ(x)⟩",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; q3[label="Kernel |⟨φ(x)|φ(y)⟩|²\\n(quantum circuit)",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; q4[label="Classical optimiser\\n→ hyperplane"]; q1->q2->q3->q4;}',"LR"),"Classical SVM vs QSVM pipelines – only the kernel/feature-map stage becomes quantum",6.4),
("h","4. Summary (book p.36)"),
("b",["Classical SVM is currently **more practical** for small-to-moderate datasets because of mature frameworks and hardware.","QSVM is **promising** for high-dimensional feature spaces and quantum-advantageous problems, but needs reliable, error-corrected quantum hardware.","As hardware improves, QSVMs may become competitive for larger datasets and specialised applications."]),
("concl","Classical SVM wins today on reliability and maturity; QSVM offers exponentially rich feature spaces and possible speed-ups, limited at present by noisy, small quantum hardware."),
("tip","**“Classical = kernel trick on CPU; Quantum = feature map in Hilbert space.”** Compare under: Representation, Effectiveness, Speed, Resources, Maturity."),
])
# ======================= 2.1 QSVM =======================
A['2.1']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.31–35 (QSVM: quantum states for data, U(θ), margin, measurement and classification, Qiskit code, circle-dataset result), p.22 (QSVM overview), p.36 (comparison)",blocks=[
("def","A **Quantum Support Vector Machine (QSVM)** is the quantum version of the classical SVM. Data points are represented as **quantum states (qubits)**, and **quantum operations** are used to find the **optimal hyperplane** that separates classes with the **maximum margin** (QAI-ii.pdf p.31)."),
("h","1. Background – classical SVM in one line"),
("b",["Find hyperplane w·x + b = 0 that maximises the margin 2/‖w‖; non-linear data are handled by the kernel trick K(x, y) = φ(x)·φ(y)."]),
("h","2. Working of QSVM (book p.31–33)"),
("n",["**Quantum data encoding:** each data point x is mapped into a quantum state |ψ_in⟩ = |φ(x)⟩ by a **quantum feature map** (e.g. second-order expansion / ZZ feature map with rotation and entangling gates).",
"**Quantum operations:** a parameterised unitary U(θ) is applied: |ψ_out⟩ = U(θ)|ψ_in⟩. Parameters θ are optimised so that the separation of the classes is best (maximum margin).",
"**Quantum kernel (alternative view):** the overlap K(x, y) = |⟨φ(x)|φ(y)⟩|² of two encoded points is estimated on the quantum computer and given to a classical SVM solver.",
"**Measurement and classification:** |ψ_out⟩ is measured with operator M; outcomes z are converted to class labels for new data points."]),
("d",dg('a[label="Classical data x"]; b[label="Quantum feature map\\n|φ(x)⟩",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; c[label="U(θ) / quantum kernel\\n|⟨φ(x)|φ(y)⟩|²",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; d[label="Measurement M\\n→ outcome z"]; e[label="Label +1 / −1",shape=oval]; f[label="Classical optimiser\\n(maximise margin, update θ)",fillcolor="#FFF2CC"]; a->b->c->d->e; d->f->c[constraint=false];',"LR"),"Working of a QSVM (hybrid quantum–classical)",6.3),
("p","**Blocks:** the feature map embeds data in a 2ⁿ-dimensional Hilbert space; U(θ)/kernel circuit does the quantum computation; measurement gives classical outcomes; the classical optimiser adjusts θ to maximise the margin."),
("h","3. Formulas"),
("f",["Margin = 2/‖w‖ (maximise);  constraints yᵢ(w·φ(xᵢ) + b) ≥ 1","Quantum kernel:  K(xᵢ, xⱼ) = |⟨φ(xᵢ)|φ(xⱼ)⟩|²","Prediction:  ŷ = sign( Σᵢ αᵢ yᵢ K(xᵢ, x) + b )"]),
("p","Symbols: w = normal vector, b = bias, yᵢ = label (±1), αᵢ = weights of support vectors, φ = feature map."),
("h","4. Example (book p.32–34)"),
("b",["2-D points; label **+1 if x₁² + x₂² < 0.5** (inside a circle), else −1 – not linearly separable.","Qiskit QSVM with a second-order feature map (depth 2), trained on 10 points, simulator backend with 1024 shots.","Predicted labels [1 −1 1 −1 1 −1] = true labels → **accuracy 100 %** on this simple dataset."]),
("h","5. Advantages and limitations"),
("b",["**Advantages:** exponentially large feature space, ability to separate complex patterns, potential speed-up.","**Limitations:** data encoding cost, many shots needed, noise on NISQ devices; for small data classical SVM is still more practical (book p.36)."]),
("concl","QSVM encodes data into quantum states, uses quantum circuits/kernels to find the maximum-margin separator, and classifies by measurement."),
("tip","**“Encode (feature map) – Kernel/U(θ) – Measure – Classify.”** Mention the circle example."),
])
# ======================= 2.5 VQA =======================
A['2.5']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.35–36 (4.11 Variational quantum algorithms: concept, 7 steps), p.15–16 (case studies: VQA for job-shop scheduling, QAOA), p.20–21 (VQE, QAOA)",blocks=[
("def","**Variational Quantum Algorithms (VQAs)** combine the power of quantum and classical computing to solve difficult optimisation problems. A **parameterised quantum circuit (ansatz)** prepares a state |ψ(θ)⟩, an **objective (cost) function** is measured, and a **classical optimiser** updates θ until the cost is minimised (QAI-ii.pdf p.35)."),
("h","1. Steps of a VQA (book p.36)"),
("n",["**Choose an ansatz** – a quantum circuit structure parameterised by variables θ.","**Initialise** the parameters θ with some values.","**Prepare the state** |ψ(θ)⟩ on the quantum computer using the current θ.","**Evaluate the objective function** E(θ) = ⟨ψ(θ)|H|ψ(θ)⟩ by repeated measurement.","**Classical optimisation:** update θ to minimise (or maximise) E(θ).","**Repeat** steps 3–5 until convergence.","**Output:** final θ* and state |ψ(θ*)⟩ give the (approximate) solution."]),
("d",dg('a[label="Choose ansatz U(θ)\\n+ initial θ"]; b[label="Quantum computer:\\nprepare |ψ(θ)⟩",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; c[label="Measure cost\\nE(θ) = ⟨H⟩"]; d[label="Classical optimiser\\nθ ← θ − η∇E",fillcolor="#FFF2CC"]; e[label="Converged?",shape=diamond]; f[label="θ*, |ψ(θ*)⟩\\nsolution",shape=oval]; a->b->c->d->e; e->b[label="no"]; e->f[label="yes"];',"LR"),"Hybrid quantum–classical loop of a VQA",6.4),
("p","**Blocks:** the ansatz is the trainable model; the quantum computer only prepares and measures (short circuits); the classical optimiser (gradient descent, SPSA, COBYLA) does the learning; the loop stops at convergence."),
("h","2. Important VQAs"),
("t",["Algorithm","Cost function / use"],[["**VQE** – Variational Quantum Eigensolver","Minimise energy ⟨H⟩ → ground state of molecules (chemistry)"],["**QAOA** – Quantum Approximate Optimisation Algorithm","Alternating problem and mixer layers → Max-Cut, graph partitioning, TSP, job-shop scheduling"],["**VQC / QNN** – variational classifier","Loss on labelled data → classification"]],[2.8,4.0]),
("h","3. Case study (book p.15–16)"),
("p","Job-shop scheduling (assign jobs to machines minimising total completion time) solved with **QAOA**: the study found solutions up to **~10 % better** than classical heuristics, even with a small number of qubits."),
("h","4. Advantages and challenges"),
("b",["**Advantages:** shallow circuits suit noisy **NISQ** hardware; noise-resilient through optimisation; flexible for chemistry, optimisation and ML.","**Challenges:** **barren plateaus** (vanishing gradients), local minima, many measurement shots, choice of ansatz."]),
("concl","VQAs let a quantum circuit evaluate a cost while a classical optimiser tunes its parameters – the most practical approach for today's quantum computers."),
("tip","**“Ansatz – Prepare – Measure – Optimise – Repeat.”** Examples: VQE, QAOA, VQC."),
])
