_ins('2.2',[("h","5. Illustration on the same dataset"),("p","For the book's circle dataset (label +1 inside x₁² + x₂² < 0.5): a classical SVM needs a well-chosen non-linear kernel (e.g. RBF) and tuned hyper-parameters; the QSVM's second-order quantum feature map separates it directly, reaching 100 % test accuracy on the simulator (QAI-ii.pdf p.34). On real NISQ hardware, shot noise and gate errors would lower QSVM accuracy, whereas the classical SVM result is exact and reproducible – exactly the trade-off of the case study.")])
_ins('2.5',[("h","5. Simple ansatz example"),("p","Two qubits: RY(θ₁) on q₀, RY(θ₂) on q₁, then CNOT(q₀→q₁). Measuring ⟨Z⊗Z⟩ gives E(θ₁, θ₂); gradient descent with the **parameter-shift rule** (∂E/∂θ = [E(θ + π/2) − E(θ − π/2)]/2) changes θ₁, θ₂ until E is minimal. Only 2 gates deep – easily run on noisy hardware, which is why VQAs are called **NISQ-friendly**.")])
